## R1. THE BIJECTION D_κ (route a) — re-derived, correct

### R1.1 Well-definedness on 𝒟

𝒟 := {(Γ_j)_{0≤j≤μ} ∈ K^{μ+1} : Γ_μ = 1}; D_κ(Γ)_j = Γ_j·ϑ_{μ−j}^{−1}.
Top coordinate: D_κ(Γ)_μ = Γ_μ·ϑ_0^{−1}. WZ-2(c) gives ϑ_0 = η^{W_κ(0)} = η^0 = 1,
so D_κ(Γ)_μ = 1 and **D_κ maps 𝒟 into 𝒟** ✓. The displayed two-sided inverse
(c_j) ↦ (c_j·ϑ_{μ−j}) is correct (ϑ_t ∈ K^×) ✓. Each ϑ_t^{−1}· is a bijection of
K fixing 0 and preserving K^×, so the occupied-slot pattern is preserved ✓ — and
this is exactly the property J-D0's NONCHAR mode (an *additive* slot shift)
lacks, which is why the note's continued use of the NONCHAR quotation is
coherent rather than self-undermining.

`#D_κ^{−1}(S) = #S` for every S ⊆ 𝒟 is immediate from bijectivity of a map of a
finite set ✓. #PP = |K| − 1 needs s′ ↦ (T − s′)^μ injective on K^×; true in every
characteristic including p | μ (Frobenius is injective), as the note says ✓.

### R1.2 Walking the consumption sites §4(b) rewires

Two sites are named in the amended §3.6 consequence paragraph.

* **Letters** — GENHN:903 `   GENHN-2), the same count as the node. Letters: |K| − 1 choices of`.
  Read in context (GENHN L898–904, layer 3: *"The map on digit spaces is
  triangular … onto by cardinality: the refine slice fixes the μ pinned digits
  and frees exactly the slots above the node floors (slot strings are
  key-independent — GENHN-2)"*), **the counted objects are digit strings, not raw
  polynomials** — so the counted set is a subset of 𝒟 at a fixed (node, κ) and
  the count factors through D_κ exactly. The set counted under WZ-PIN is
  D_κ^{−1}(PP), under the twisted reading it is PP; both have |K| − 1 elements ✓.
* **The bracket** b_{μ,K}(κ) = (|K|−1)|K|^{(c(μ)+1)κ−1} (GENHN L930). Only the
  (|K|−1) factor is convention-sensitive; the |K|^{(c(μ)+1)κ−1} factor counts free
  slots above the node floors, which are *height* conditions, convention-blind by
  inspection. The free-slot factor is strictly outside 𝒟 = K^{μ+1}, so the cited
  lemma does not literally cover it — but the extension is a trivial product of
  bijections, and the note has already named the premise that makes (iii) reach
  the count laws as the hostile arc's target 4 (*"that what GENHN-4 counts at a
  fixed (node, κ) is in bijection with 𝒟, with the counted predicate a function
  of the digit vector alone"*) and again at §5.1(3) (*"the census equality is
  what (iii) predicts when the member set fibres uniformly over the digit space …
  this note does not re-derive that fibration"*). **Disclosed premise, not a
  hidden one — no finding.**

### R1.3 The sharp dichotomy — checked at every (e₁, i(κ))

The brief's worry (⌊i(κ)/e₁⌋ can be 0 with η ≠ 1) is the right question and the
chain survives it, because ⌊i(κ)/e₁⌋ is **always** 0: the normalizer convention
fixes 0 ≤ i(κ) < e₁, so ϑ_1 = η^0 = 1 *for every* (e₁, i(κ), η). The forcing runs
in the sound direction — the hypothesis is ϑ_t^{−1} = ξ^{−t} for all t, t = 1
*forces* ξ = ϑ_1 = 1, and then ϑ_t = ξ^t = 1 for every t ≤ μ. No case is lost.
σ_ξ's slot action re-derived independently: [y^j](ξ^{−deg R}R(ξy)) = c_j ξ^{j−deg R},
i.e. the height-tκ slot (t = μ−j) is multiplied by ξ^{−t} ✓. Equality of the two
maps on 𝒟 is testable slot-by-slot because 𝒟 contains vectors with every slot
nonzero ✓. Converse ϑ ≡ 1 ⟹ D_κ = σ_1 = id ✓. **The dichotomy is right.**

