## R2. THE F₃ TOOTH (route b) — hand-checked, correct

Frame realizability: K = F₃ ⟹ q = 3, f₁ = 1; η = 2 ∈ K^× is available; e₁ = 2,
h odd ⟹ i(κ) = κ mod 2, so i(κ) = 1 at κ odd; κ > D′h = 2h is satisfiable. ✓

ϑ_t = η^{⌊t·1/2⌋}: ϑ = (1, 1, 2) ✓ as displayed.
R_N = (T−1)² = T² − 2T + 1 = T² + T + 1 over F₃ ⟹ c = (1,1,1) ✓.
Γ_j = c_j·ϑ_{μ−j} ⟹ Γ = (2,1,1) ⟹ R_Γ = T² + T + 2 ✓.
disc = 1 − 8 ≡ 2 (mod 3), a non-square; direct root test T = 0,1,2 gives 2,1,2 —
no roots, **inert** ✓.

The four-vector display also checks: multiplicative perfect squares
{(1,1,1),(1,2,1)} (= (T−1)², (T−2)²); their D_κ-preimages {(2,1,1),(2,2,1)};
coherent perfect squares {(1,1,1),(1,2,1)}; their D_κ-images {(2,1,1),(2,2,1)}
(ϑ_2 = 2 is its own inverse in F₃, so image set = preimage set here). Both
crossed vectors are inert: T²+T+2 (above) and T²+2T+2 (disc 4−8 ≡ 2; values
2,2,1 at T = 0,1,2) ✓. So the two sets are disjoint, each of size |K| − 1 = 2 ✓
— "counts blind, membership not" is exactly right. ("All four" counts the two
distinct vectors twice, once in each role; loose wording, not an error.)

**The tooth is real, and §5.1 shows it firing 972 times in sealed data (R3).**

